{"input": "Summarize their discussions on university mergers.", "answers": ["Kirsty Williams firstly proposed that the main goal he would like to pursue was a healthy, strong and sustainable HE sector. They did not pursue or urge a policy of mergers, but they welcomed the mergers, if this was beneficial for individual institutions. Hefin David proposed that was in contrast with the predecessors, and talked about the situation when Cardiff Metropolitan University was under pressure from the previous Minister to merge with the University of Glamorgan and Newport. Kirsty Williams thought they needed to look at the future instead of looking at the past. They had a strong institution in Cardiff Met now. However, it was obvious that they got to learn some lessons from that process."], "length": 13868, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Cabinet Secretary for Education, Kirsty Williams, stated that she is not in favor of pursuing a policy of mergers, but is open to collaboration between institutions. She mentioned that Cardiff Metropolitan University had a program with Cardiff University for teacher training, and that there are new partnerships and collaborations between institutions. She also stated that the decision to merge institutions is a matter for the institutions themselves. The Chair, Lynne Neagle, asked if there were any disadvantages to Cardiff Metropolitan University as a result of the merger, and the Cabinet Secretary stated that she was not aware of any disadvantages. The Chair also asked if the Cabinet Secretary was concerned about the long-term sustainability of the University of South Wales, and the Cabinet Secretary stated that she was aware of concerns, but did not have any information on the current situation."}
